WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.810
There are a lot of films that probably come
to mind when you think about superheroes.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:11.070
The genre covers an insane range of movies,
but a special few definitely stand out above

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.070
the rest.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.730
From the Marvel and DC universes to a few
outside-the-box superhero films, these are

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.220
the best superhero movies you absolutely must
see.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.550
The Avengers

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.941
The Marvel Cinematic Universe currently spans
more than a dozen films and counting, but

00:00:27.941 --> 00:00:33.610
the pinnacle of the franchise was arguably
established relatively early on with The Avengers.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:38.860
The studio tapped geek demigod Joss Whedon
to write and direct the project, and the result

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.540
was a critical and commercial hit.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.330
The Avengers remains one of the most successful
films ever made.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:50.980
The movie pulled in more than $1.5 billion
worldwide and is still one of the highest

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.360
rated films in the MCU.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:57.330
It's a comic book brought to life in the best
possible way thanks to Whedon's ability to

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:03.200
blend a bevy of characters and a compelling
story into one perfect package.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.410
The Dark Knight

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:09.940
Christopher Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy is
widely considered one of the best Batman interpretations

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.260
ever put to screen.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.510
It's been more than a decade since the trilogy
launched, so it's easy to forget just how

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.430
significant these movies were for the franchise.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:25.330
Eight years after Batman &amp; Robin bombed, Nolan
proved the Caped Crusader was still viable

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:31.110
on the big screen with Batman Begins...then
raised the stakes even higher with 2008's

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.090
The Dark Knight, arguably the best of the
bunch.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:39.450
The film is moody, smart, atmospheric, and
boasted some of the best superhero action

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.100
scenes ever put to film.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.470
Heath Ledger's performance as the Joker is
a particularly fine example of the genre's

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:46.600
dramatic potential.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:53.159
"Never start with the head the victim gets
all fuzzy you can't feel the…"

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:55.620
"See?"

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.380
Captain America: Civil War

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:02.570
This Captain America sequel has been described
as Avengers 2.5, and for good reason.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.110
It featured as many heroes as your typical
Avengers film, and told a politically charged

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:12.480
story that set Earth's Mightiest Heroes against
one another in one of the most eye-popping

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.620
big-screen battles ever conceived.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.779
Along with giving fans high-stakes hero vs.
hero battles, Civil War also took a major

00:02:19.779 --> 00:02:22.540
step toward moving the MCU forward.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:27.800
It introduced Chadwick Boseman's Black Panther
to mainstream audiences, and gave us our first

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.709
look at Tom Holland's newest incarnation of
Spider-Man.

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:33.989
"I coulda stuck the landing a little better
it's just the new suit.

00:02:35.829 --> 00:02:38.200
"Yea yea, we really don't need to.. start
a conversation."

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:38.940
"Ok ok."

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:39.800
"Cap..

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.200
Captain."

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.200
The Incredibles

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.739
We're calling it right here and now: The Incredibles
is one of the best superhero movies ever.

00:02:46.739 --> 00:02:47.739
"Honey…"

00:02:47.739 --> 00:02:48.739
"What…"

00:02:48.739 --> 00:02:51.139
"Where is my super suit?"

00:02:51.139 --> 00:02:52.139
"What?"

00:02:52.139 --> 00:02:54.920
"Where is my super suit!?"

00:02:54.920 --> 00:03:00.439
Brad Bird's 2004 animated masterpiece was
basically a spin on the classic Fantastic

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:03.450
Four story, but with so much more.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:08.840
It built a fascinating world and seamlessly
grappled with questions of adulthood and responsibility

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.200
in ways kids and grown-ups could follow and
appreciate.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.060
It's also crazy fun and laugh-out-loud hilarious.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.180
"Oh."

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:28.379
It's taken almost 15 years, but a sequel is
finally in the works with a release date set

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:29.920
for 2019.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.400
Does The Incredibles need a sequel?

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.569
No, but having Brad Bird back in the saddle
helps ease our fears.

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:37.569
A little.

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:39.029
Iron Man

00:03:39.029 --> 00:03:44.290
This movie is the granddaddy of the MCU, and
it's just as good today as it was when it

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.180
blew our socks off in 2008.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.220
With heavy hitters like the X-Men and Spider-Man
already sold off to larger studios, Marvel

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:03.089
had to pull heroes from the bench to launch
its shared universe.

00:04:03.089 --> 00:04:08.079
Iron Man was one of the best of the rest,
so they gave director Jon Favreau a lot of

00:04:08.079 --> 00:04:11.769
freedom and banked on the charisma of Robert
Downey, Jr.

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:17.340
The gamble worked, and this movie managed
to tell an extremely fun story while setting

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:20.049
the tone for the dozen or so movies to follow.

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:26.699
It's also a textbook example of how to get
a superhero origin story right on film.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:27.699
Deadpool

00:04:27.699 --> 00:04:33.840
It's an absolute miracle this blood-soaked,
F-bomb-drenched film ever made it out of development

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:35.900
hell, but aren't we all glad it did?

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.870
A Deadpool movie kicked around in script form
for years, but it took a leaked concept reel

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:44.169
to finally build enough momentum to get it
made.

00:04:44.169 --> 00:04:49.729
The Merc With A Mouth now holds the record
for the highest-grossing X-Men film and the

00:04:49.729 --> 00:04:52.419
highest-grossing R-rated film in history.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:56.700
Not bad for a B-list comic character with
a foul mouth.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.660
"That does it!"

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.340
"Oh.. ah ah...Canada!"

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.780
X-Men: Days of Future Past

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.640
Combining the worlds of the original X-Men
trilogy with the prequel-sequel X-Men: First

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:18.069
Class was a tall order, but Bryan Singer pulled
it off in spades with his return to the franchise.

00:05:18.069 --> 00:05:23.419
Days of Future Past managed to deftly weave
together a story spanning decades, with two

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:28.710
separate casts, and still keep all those narrative
threads tight enough that even casual fans

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.020
could still follow along.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:36.210
It also stealthily retconned the disappointing
X-Men: The Last Stand, making all things right

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.319
within the cinematic X-universe.

00:05:39.319 --> 00:05:41.120
Not bad, right?

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.580
Blade 2

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:48.480
When the first Blade film, released in 1998,
turned out to be a surprise box office hit,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.159
the studio brought in Guillermo del Toro to
tackle the sequel.

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:56.850
Blade 2 was basically everything you loved
about the first film, but better.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:01.539
Instead of just fighting bloodsuckers, day
walker Blade is roped into a larger fight

00:06:01.539 --> 00:06:05.659
for survival against the positively terrifying
Reapers.

00:06:05.659 --> 00:06:10.689
Much like X-Men, this film was an early proving
ground for serious adaptations of superhero

00:06:10.689 --> 00:06:16.060
stories that predated The Avengers and their
pals by several years.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:17.289
Logan

00:06:17.289 --> 00:06:23.560
After the unanticipated mega-success of the
R-rated Deadpool, 2017's Logan finally allowed

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:29.229
Jackman's spirit animal to spill copious amounts
of blood, sever limbs, and make full use of

00:06:29.229 --> 00:06:32.509
the colorful vocabulary he's long enjoyed
in the comics.

00:06:32.509 --> 00:06:37.800
Pitched as basically "Little Miss Sunshine
with superheroes," Logan is a movie that X-Men

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:41.110
fans never expected to actually happen.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:46.020
Inspired by the classic Western Shane and
very loosely based on Mark Millar's Old Man

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:51.840
Logan comics, Logan makes Wolverine interesting
again as a broken man with a failing healing

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:52.840
factor.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.759
Sir Patrick Stewart gives one of the best
performances of his career, but it's Dafne

00:06:56.759 --> 00:07:03.110
Keen's performance as the merciless X-23 that
elevates a would-be grim character study into

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:06.129
must-see neo-Western murder-pageant.

00:07:06.129 --> 00:07:07.900
Guardians of the Galaxy

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.560
This movie is just crazy fun.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.789
Chris Pratt's Star-Lord proves that '70s rock
songs belong in outer space—and so do Marvel

00:07:15.789 --> 00:07:16.789
superheroes.

00:07:16.789 --> 00:07:21.770
Zoe Saldana enchants as the green-skinned
assassin with a conscience, Gamora.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:26.150
Bradley Cooper shows his impressive range
as the voice of Rocket Raccoon, and Vin Diesel's

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.900
Groot makes three words speak volumes.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.520
"I am Groot"

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:35.789
"Yea, that's as fascinating as the first 89
times you told me that."

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:40.840
Sure, Thanos's powers seem limited to interdimensional
conference calls and brooding in his floating

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:46.570
space chair, and Ronan the Accuser's actions
defy common understanding of the Infinity

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:47.570
Stones.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:53.310
But none of these discrepancies really matter,
because it's just so much fun to watch.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:56.300
"What are you doing?"

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.719
I'm distracting ya, ya big turd blossom."

00:07:58.719 --> 00:07:59.719
Thanks for watching!

00:07:59.719 --> 00:08:02.189
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:02.189 --> 00:08:05.389
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

